  "findings": [
    {
      "heading": "Running initial analyses",
      "segment_index": "S9.P1",
      "kind": "untrue",
      "en_span": "chapters/survival_analysis.qmd:257-257",
      "tr_span": "chapters/survival_analysis.fr.qmd:251-251",
      "proposition": "English defines n.risk as people who have neither had the event yet NOR been censored; the French says 'les personnes qui n'ont pas encore eu l'evenement OU qui ont ete censurees', i.e. people who have not yet had the event OR who have been censored, which includes censored people in the risk set.",
      "reader_consequence": "A French reader learns a wrong definition of the risk set: they will believe censored individuals are still counted in n.risk, and will misread every life table and Kaplan-Meier step produced in the rest of the chapter.",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Running initial analyses",
      "segment_index": "S9.P1",
      "kind": "untrue",
      "en_span": "chapters/survival_analysis.qmd:262-262",
      "tr_span": "chapters/survival_analysis.fr.qmd:256-256",
      "proposition": "English says the estimates being fitted are the KM (Kaplan-Meier) estimates; the French says 'les estimations de la GC', naming an abbreviation 'GC' that is defined nowhere in the chapter and that the English does not support.",
      "reader_consequence": "The French reader cannot connect this fitting step to the Kaplan-Meier estimator introduced two paragraphs earlier, and is left with an undefined quantity 'GC' at the point where the central model of the chapter is fitted.",
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Running initial analyses",
      "segment_index": "S9.P2",
      "kind": "code_mismatch",
      "en_span": "chapters/survival_analysis.qmd:275-275",
      "tr_span": "chapters/survival_analysis.fr.qmd:269-269",
      "proposition": "The French says the 'times' option specifies 'certaines heures' (certain hours), but the chunk beside it is summary(linelistsurv_fit, times = c(5,10,20,30,60)) and futime was built as as.double(date_outcome - date_onset), i.e. days, not hours.",
      "reader_consequence": "The French reader reads the printed survival summary as being at 5, 10, 20, 30 and 60 hours of follow-up instead of days, and misstates the time scale of every survival probability quoted from that output.",
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Fitting a Cox model",
      "segment_index": "S15.P3",
      "kind": "untrue",
      "en_span": "chapters/survival_analysis.qmd:544-544",
      "tr_span": "chapters/survival_analysis.fr.qmd:538-538",
      "proposition": "English says the 'method' argument determines how TIES are handled (efron, breslow, exact); the French says it determines 'comment les liens sont traites', i.e. how links are handled.",
      "reader_consequence": "The French reader does not learn that efron/breslow/exact are tie-handling rules for coincident event times, so they cannot judge when the default matters in data with many equal follow-up times.",
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Resources",
